# The device Destroy methods that release memory held outside virtual
# memory, and which tests/check-device-destroy.sh therefore holds to
# clearing each handle they release and storing the private struct back.
#
# A device is retired more than once as a matter of course: setpagedevice
# retires the outgoing one, the interpreter's shutdown retires the current
# one, and the device is an element of the graphics state, so a restore
# past a setpagedevice makes a retired device current again. Every one of
# those paths runs the same method on the same instance, which is why
# src/lib/xpost_dev_driver.h makes Destroy idempotent: it clears each
# resource handle in the private struct and stores the struct back, so a
# repeated Destroy is a no-op rather than a double free.
#
# A line is "<route> <file> <function>". The route is how the check
# reached it, and is derived rather than chosen:
#
#   table  the function the Destroy slot of a device method table names.
#          A compiled device may not install a method slot outside its
#          table -- tests/check-device-skeleton.sh refuses that -- so the
#          tables are the whole of this route.
#   class  the function behind an operator that a PostScript device
#          class calls from its /Destroy body.
#
# A class dictionary that is a copy of another's is not a line of its own,
# because the copy carries the original's Destroy: the DSC writer is such
# a copy of the PDF writer, and tests/check-vecbase.sh holds it to staying
# one.

entries 7

# ---- the compiled devices ----

table src/lib/xpost_dev_bgr.c _destroy
table src/lib/xpost_dev_jpeg.c _destroy
table src/lib/xpost_dev_png.c _destroy
table src/lib/xpost_dev_raster.c _destroy
table src/lib/xpost_dev_win32.c _destroy
table src/lib/xpost_dev_xcb.c _destroy

# ---- the vector writers, which release through an operator ----
#
# The PDF and SVG classes keep their page content in a malloc'd
# accumulator rather than in PostScript objects, and give it up through
# .pdffree. The same function is the release the interpreter performs on
# a retired PostScript device, where no procedure can be run.

class src/lib/xpost_dev_generic.c _pdffree

# ---- the classes with nothing to release ----
#
# A device class whose raster is PostScript objects holds nothing outside
# virtual memory: the collector owns every byte of it, and the right
# Destroy is the bare pop that drops the instance and returns. Those
# classes are exempt from everything above, and are counted here so that a
# new one cannot join them without saying so.
#
#   data/bboxdev.ps   accumulates a bounding box in the instance; marks no
#                     pixels and allocates nothing.
#   data/image.ps     the base class every raster class derives from: its
#                     rows are PostScript strings under /ImgData.
#   data/nulldev.ps   the minimal conforming device; it holds no state at
#                     all.

exemptions 3

exempt data/bboxdev.ps
exempt data/image.ps
exempt data/nulldev.ps

# ---- and the count that is not a dial ----
#
# The number of Destroys that release a handle and leave it in the private
# struct, or clear one and do not store the struct back. It is zero, and
# the check refuses any other value: raising it would record a double free
# rather than remove one.

unsafe 0
